[Music]  hey everybody welcome to the first part  of the making of planet coaster and just  to clarify by that I don't mean the game  itself but a roller coaster which I made  in the game and released a couple of  days ago thankfully I saved some time  lapses of its because I got so many  requests to turn this into a how it's  made type of series that I figured I  could take these time lapses and  actually make this two-part video series  out of it and in this series I'll just  show you how I made the coaster itself  how I make the planet that it revolves  around and how I construct the entire  idea that somehow it's out there up in  space when in reality it's all just in  game or most of it is at least so  without further ado let's get into the  time-lapse so of course the thing that I  had to start with is making the planet  itself and I did this in a way which is  very straight forward but in hindsight I  think there could have been easier ways  if you want to do this yourself one  thing which you could do is actually  make a perfect sphere out of scenery and  then terraform around that in this case  though I just decided to take the  terraforming tool only and just work on  a sphere just carefully changing some  things here and there until it looks  like a perfect universe fear it's  definitely not perfect in the end but  with a lot of smoothing and try on error  to get all of the bumps out there it's  more or less enough of a ball to turn it  into an actual planet the characteristic  then I feel matters most about the  planet is the size of it I wanted to  make sure that it was large enough to  actually seem like an actual microscopic  planets and large enough to fit some  buildings and trees on without set  buildings and trees completely touring  the planet itself while at the same time  making it small enough to realistically  well not really realistically but to  allow me to actually build a coaster  around it if it were much larger it'd be  hard to have the coast to revolve around  the planet without losing too much speed  and also it would take much more time to  do this entire project and in the end I  think  in game it took around 20 hours spread  over two days as it was just a small  Christmas vacation kind of project and  I'm actually really happy with how  little time it took this build and most  of my time was actually spent in sort of  creating the illusion that it's much  more than it really is whenever unity  it's just a floating bubble of  terraforming with a bunch of trees  around it so going almost into the  coastal layout here I was pretty  confident that I could make the course  the layouts in the way that I wanted  since the sphere is quite small because  the way that I wanted to make the  coastal layouts is in that kind of  confusing way where it's not quite clear  what's up and what's down because  obviously this planet being in space you  kind of do want to get rid of the idea  that there is up and down at all and  this was very challenging I didn't  really have any kind of planning before  I went into making this layout because I  had no clue how to prepare for making a  coastal layouts around super small  planets it's not exactly something that  I've done before but the one thing that  I did want to make sure I did with this  layout is to make the riders lose their  orientation and that's why I decided  that it would be best to make this  layout out of large sweeping curves that  don't change their direction too much  and mostly stay very close to the ground  of the planet itself and you can see  this in the first curve already there  the only reason why it goes completely  to the bottom of the planet is because I  wanted to start off the ride with the  zero-g roll already like lose all of  your orientation what exactly is where  since the zero during that entire zero-g  roll it starts going inverted already  which is already kind of confusing since  the zero zero of course banks 360  degrees but also those 360 degrees  you're upside down which is already kind  of weird and that is more or less the  pollock short for this entire station  thing that you're coming out of and you  also already make quite a bit of speed  during that but after that first curve  coming up again I wanted to make the  small upwards launching L AM launched  parts just to make sure that you don't  lose  your momentum I wanted to make the  entire ride quite fast and it's a pretty  easy way to make it yeah just to make  the coaster not lose any speed and to  make sure that it can stay close to the  planet itself at all times and also one  thing that I wanted to make sure with  this is that it has enough speed at the  end to make its way back to the station  without having to add another little to  the top of the planets because that  would obviously disrupt any idea that  there is no up and down on this planet  so that was quite important and then  after that li m launch I figured I would  have a section of the coaster with two  very large sweeping curves and we're  working on the second one over here and  basically what I wanted to do with this  is half these curves that are mostly  horizontal because one thing that  obviously gives away what's the bottom  and what's the top of this planet is the  fact that the cars will always gravitate  toward the bottom of the planet so I  wanted to have some curves that seem  like they're horizontal curves but are  actually kind of diagonal curves and the  cars don't really accelerate too much  over the course of these curves to make  it very obvious where up and down is at  least that's what I was going for and I  hope I kind of succeeded in that and  instead the cars just have a very even  fast speed going very close to the  planets and then the final two elements  are these large helixes which are almost  exactly vertical which kind of make it  seem as is as if the vertical side of  this planet is actually horizontal these  are technically just loops but they're  banked outward to make it seem as if  they are helixes and one of the things  which kind of helps with this idea is  these little airtime Hills that aren't  actually airtime Hills they're actually  Hills then extend outward and would give  some lethal G's in real life but in this  case it kind of makes it look as if the  ending of the ride is a bunch of helixes  with airtime Hills and that basically  concludes the ride which has three basic  parts I'd say with the first part being  the zero-g gyros and the launch section  the second one being those large  sweeping  curves where it's hard to tell on what  side of the planet you are really at and  lost point being those two helixes and  after that I wanted to move into making  the station of the coaster and this was  all just a lot of stuff that I was  winging at the time I didn't really  think it was too important to get into  the designs of the the station and the  other console at this point the only  thing that I had prepared for myself was  that I wanted to have two consoles on  this planet the reason why I wanted to  have two is because one console would  very easily give away what's the top and  what's the bottom of the console and  exactly removed that suspense of  disbelief that this is an actual planet  so I wanted to have two castles and this  also gives the planet a very nice kind  of duality structure to it it actually  seems a lot like yin yang if I look back  at it with one side you know this being  the daytime side and the other side  being the nighttime side and they're  both kind of separated by these large  sweeping curves of the coaster itself  which is very interesting but there's  definitely a duality in the planet in  that sense not just because it looks  good but also because it removes the  idea that you know there is up and down  it just makes it seem more like you can  go anywhere on this thing and because  there's such a duality between the two  halves of the planet I also wanted to  make sure that these two castles  are similar in a way but also very  distinct so the top castle here I wanted  to mostly use the domes in the game and  make it more of a wide building than it  really is tall so it's mostly just  characterized by this very large dome  which sits just on top of the station  and then there's of course the domed  kind of onion tower right next to it  which and we're looking a lot like a  mosque at this point which is something  that I did kind of want to avoid I just  wanted to make it look a bit more  neutral kind of fantasy esque so I hope  with adding a lot of these different  gothic elements to it around all the  detailing  especially the windows and the doors and  the arches that it's it's a bit less  pinpoint of all  any kind of location or inspiration it's  really just a castle for the sake of  there being a castle on top of the  planet and I also put these battlements  on the side or um they're not called  battlements the battlements set of  things on top of the walls I put these  buttresses on the side of the building  which are a bit of a gothic touch as  well they're really just for fun because  they don't actually support anything and  they're structurally completely  illogical but it's it's a nice touch of  detail and I wanted to add into the East  station I mean it's not like this  builder is very logical to begin with  and they decide I'm actually using a  surprising amount of just simple in-game  elements and not really making anything  myself since I didn't want to spend too  much time on this castle it's not really  focus to make the castle Lukas detailed  or intricate or unique itself it's  really just a build that I wanted to  have on top of this planet to shape the  look of the planet as a whole  so it's it's quite simple and I put a  bunch of rocks and foliage around its to  detail it and blend it in a little bit  with the rest of the planets as well  what was a little bit tricky about the  castle is 